dsh: reasoning:
The rev29 change is exactly what the life05 repair described:
1. D5 definition: "strictly STRONGER" → EQUIVALENT (past-closedness argument).
2. visibility.definition: removed misclassification example, states tail/whole equivalence.
3. variant SET relation: "strictly STRONGER" → "strictly WEAKER".
4. f0_binding.consistency_evidence_sha256 refreshed 675a99d0d25b → 9e335e9ba1bf.
5. revision 12 → 13, revised_at updated.

Now: the key mathematical question in rev29's changes. The rev12 text said whole-curve containment is strictly STRONGER than tail containment, and the SET variant is strictly STRONGER. Rev13 says both are EQUIVALENT / tail entails set (so single-q tail is STRONGER than set union).

Let me think carefully about the math, since my job as reviewer is to check correctness:

Definitions: gamma: [0,T) → M future-inextendible causal geodesic. J^-(q) is the causal past of q (all points p such that there's a future-directed causal curve from p to q), including q presumably. Claim (rev13): J^-(q) is past-closed: if p' ≤ p (p' causally precedes p) and p ∈ J^-(q), then p' ∈ J^-(q). Yes, by transitivity of causal relation, p' ≤ p ≤ q ⟹ p' ≤ q. So past-closed. 

For a causal geodesic gamma: [0,T) → M, the image gamma([0,T)) is a causal curve; for t < t0, gamma(t) ≤ gamma(t0) (since the segment gamma|[t,t0] is a future-directed causal curve from gamma(t) to gamma(t0)). So if the tail gamma([t0,T)) ⊆ J^-(q), then for any t < t0, gamma(t) ≤ gamma(t0) ∈ J^-(q), and past-closedness gives gamma(t) ∈ J^-(q). Hence gamma([0,T)) ⊆ J^-(q) ⟺ tail ⊆ J^-(q). So the "EQUIVALENT" claim is correct **provided** (a) J^-(q) is defined as the set of points causally preceding q (the standard "causal past", with the convention that causal curves are future-directed and include the endpoint), and (b) the containment is interpreted pointwise for the entire half-open curve. Yes, this is right. Note the subtlety: for t < t0, gamma(t) ≤ gamma(t0) requires the curve from gamma(t) to gamma(t0) to be causal, which it is (segment of a causal geodesic). And past-closedness is exactly transitivity. So equivalence holds. The rev13 correction is mathematically sound.

Now variant SET: "gamma visible from I+ iff gamma([0,T)) ⊆ ∪_{q∈I+} J^-(q) ∩ M". Single-q tail predicate: ∃q∈I+, ∃t0: tail gamma([t0,T)) ⊆ J^-(q). Single-q tail ⟹ whole curve ⊆ J^-(q) (by equivalence) ⟹ whole curve ⊆ union. So single-q predicate entails the union reading. Converse fails in general (need a counterexample where the curve is in the union but no single q sees a tail; requires infinitely many q's, e.g., a curve approaching I+ such that different portions are seen by different q's). So SET is strictly WEAKER (less restrictive) than the single-q predicate. The rev13 correction: "strictly WEAKER than this class's single-q tail predicate" — correct (assuming the class's predicate is the single-q one; and note the class predicate is actually single-q TAIL, equivalent to single-q whole). Good.

But wait — one subtlety: the rev13 relation text says "the single-q tail predicate entails the union reading, and non-containment in the union implies no single q sees a tail of gamma, but not conversely". The second clause: "non-containment in the union implies no single q sees a tail" — yes since tail ⊆ single J^-(q) ⊆ union. "but not conversely": i.e., no single q sees a tail does not imply non-containment in the union. Correct.

So rev13's corrections are mathematically sound. Good — this is the kind of thing I should verify independently, and I should verify the full-schema acceptance fields.

